data: {"type": "response.output_text.annotation.added", "output_index": 4, "item_id": "id-6", "content_index": 0, "annotation_index": 0, "annotation": {"type": "url_citation", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/static/history/PDFs/great_wall_less_great_from_space.pdf", "title": "Feature", "start_index": 0, "end_index": 0, "content": "7/2/2019 NASA - China's Wall Less Great in View from Space https://www.nasa.gov/vision/space/workinginspace/great_wall.html 1/2\n\n...\n\nDisclaimer: This material is being kept online for historical purposes. Though accurate at the time of publication, it is no longer being updated. The page may contain broken links or outdated information, and parts may not function in current web browsers. Visit NASA.gov for current information\n\n...\n\nChina's Wall Less Great in View from Space 05.09.05\n\n## Feature\n\nIt has become a space-based myth. The Great Wall of China, frequently billed as the only man-made object visible from space, generally isn't, at least to the unaided eye in low Earth orbit. It certa"}, "sequence_number": 12}

data: {"type": "response.output_text.annotation.added", "output_index": 4, "item_id": "id-6", "content_index": 0, "annotation_index": 1, "annotation": {"type": "url_citation", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/great-wall/", "title": "Great Wall", "start_index": 0, "end_index": 0, "content": "# Great Wall\n\nNASA\n\nApr 02, 2009\n\nThe Great Wall of China and Inner Mongolia are featured in this image photographed by Expedition 10 Commander Leroy Chiao on the International Space Station. Despite myths to the contrary, the wall isn't visible from the moon, and is difficult or impossible to see from Earth orbit without the high-powered lenses used for this photo.\n\nThe Great Wall of China and Inner Mongolia are featured in this image photographed by Expedition 10 Commander Leroy Chiao on the International Space Station. Despite myths to the contrary, the wall isn’t visible from the moon, and is difficult or impossible to see from Earth orbit without the high-powered lenses used for this ph"}, "sequence_number": 13}

data: {"type": "response.output_text.annotation.added", "output_index": 4, "item_id": "id-6", "content_index": 0, "annotation_index": 2, "annotation": {"type": "url_citation", "url": "https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/no-you-cant-see-the-great-wall-of-china-from-space/", "title": "No, You Can’t See the Great Wall of China from Space | Scientific American", "start_index": 0, "end_index": 0, "content": "No, You Can’t See the Great Wall of China from Space | Scientific American\n\nAugust 9, 2024\n\nNo, You Can’t See the Great Wall of China from Space\n\nReports of Earth-orbiting astronauts glimpsing the Great Wall of China and many other artificial structures are wildly exaggerated, but humanity’s planetary influence isn’t entirely invisible from afar\n\nBy Phil Plait edited by Lee Billings\n\nThe Jinshanling section of the Great Wall of China. Bjdlzx/Getty Images\n\nWhen I was a mere lad, probably in middle school, I remember a friend telling me that the Apollo astronauts could see the Great Wall of China from the moon.\n\nIf someone told me that now, I’d know almost instinctively that this claim is balo"}, "sequence_number": 14}

