data: {"type": "response.output_item.done", "output_index": 4, "item": {"id": "id-6", "type": "message", "status": "completed", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "output_text", "text": "## Evidence\n\n1. **NASA — “China’s Wall Less Great in View from Space”**  \n   URL: https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/static/history/PDFs/great_wall_less_great_from_space.pdf  \n   **Passage:** “The Great Wall of China, frequently billed as the only man-made object visible from space, generally isn't, at least to the unaided eye in low Earth orbit.”  \n   **Relation:** Undermines the claim as phrased. The page also reports that an ISS photograph captured small sections, but says it was taken with a 180mm camera lens.\n\n2. **NASA — “Great Wall”**  \n   URL: https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/great-wall/  \n   **Passage:** “the wall isn't visible from the moon, and is difficult or impossible to see from Earth orbit without the high-powered lenses used for this photo.”  \n   **Relation:** Undermines the naked-eye claim; the pictured view used high-powered lenses.\n\n3. **Scientific American — “No, You Can’t See the Great Wall of China from Space”**  \n   URL: https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/no-you-cant-see-the-great-wall-of-china-from-space/  \n   **Passage:** “So even then, it’s still too small to see by eye!”  \n   **Relation:** Undermines the claim. The article adds that the wall’s low contrast with its surroundings makes it difficult to distinguish, and says orbital images showing detail use telephoto lenses.\n\n   **Passage:** “For example, when the sun is low in the sky at sunrise or sunset, the wall can cast a long shadow, revealing its presence. But that’s not really the same as seeing the wall itself, is it?”  \n   **Relation:** This describes a possible way to detect evidence of the wall from orbit, but distinguishes that from seeing the wall itself.\n\n## What could not be determined\n\nThe excerpts do not establish whether there are rare viewing conditions in which a person might discern the wall itself without optical aid. They also do not provide a full account of the observations or methods behind every astronaut report.", "annotations": [{"type": "url_citation", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/static/history/PDFs/great_wall_less_great_from_space.pdf", "title": "Feature", "start_index": 0, "end_index": 0, "content": "7/2/2019 NASA - China's Wall Less Great in View from Space https://www.nasa.gov/vision/space/workinginspace/great_wall.html 1/2\n\n...\n\nDisclaimer: This material is being kept online for historical purposes. Though accurate at the time of publication, it is no longer being updated. The page may contain broken links or outdated information, and parts may not function in current web browsers. Visit NASA.gov for current information\n\n...\n\nChina's Wall Less Great in View from Space 05.09.05\n\n## Feature\n\nIt has become a space-based myth. The Great Wall of China, frequently billed as the only man-made object visible from space, generally isn't, at least to the unaided eye in low Earth orbit. It certa"}, {"type": "url_citation", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/great-wall/", "title": "Great Wall", "start_index": 0, "end_index": 0, "content": "# Great Wall\n\nNASA\n\nApr 02, 2009\n\nThe Great Wall of China and Inner Mongolia are featured in this image photographed by Expedition 10 Commander Leroy Chiao on the International Space Station. Despite myths to the contrary, the wall isn't visible from the moon, and is difficult or impossible to see from Earth orbit without the high-powered lenses used for this photo.\n\nThe Great Wall of China and Inner Mongolia are featured in this image photographed by Expedition 10 Commander Leroy Chiao on the International Space Station. Despite myths to the contrary, the wall isn’t visible from the moon, and is difficult or impossible to see from Earth orbit without the high-powered lenses used for this ph"}, {"type": "url_citation", "url": "https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/no-you-cant-see-the-great-wall-of-china-from-space/", "title": "No, You Can’t See the Great Wall of China from Space | Scientific American", "start_index": 0, "end_index": 0, "content": "No, You Can’t See the Great Wall of China from Space | Scientific American\n\nAugust 9, 2024\n\nNo, You Can’t See the Great Wall of China from Space\n\nReports of Earth-orbiting astronauts glimpsing the Great Wall of China and many other artificial structures are wildly exaggerated, but humanity’s planetary influence isn’t entirely invisible from afar\n\nBy Phil Plait edited by Lee Billings\n\nThe Jinshanling section of the Great Wall of China. Bjdlzx/Getty Images\n\nWhen I was a mere lad, probably in middle school, I remember a friend telling me that the Apollo astronauts could see the Great Wall of China from the moon.\n\nIf someone told me that now, I’d know almost instinctively that this claim is balo"}], "logprobs": []}]}, "sequence_number": 464}

